/* lights.lps — a program you can click on.
 *
 * §I.10.4d: an animation can be an interface rather than a picture. A program
 * that defines `lps_mousedown/3`, `lps_mouseup/3` or `lps_mousedrag/3` gets
 * those events injected from a live 2D or 3D window, carrying the scene
 * coordinates of the pointer. A program that does not define them gets nothing
 * — no listener is even attached, so a click on a picture stays a click on a
 * picture.
 *
 * Four lamps in a row. Click one to toggle it; the safety rule refuses to leave
 * the room in the dark, and the constraint is what stops it rather than the
 * click handler being careful.
 *
 * To try it:
 *
 *   1. ./lps ide          and open this file
 *   2. Live session ▸ Start
 *   3. press 2D — the button is enabled because this program has display/2
 *   4. click the lamps in the window that opens
 *
 * From the command line, `./lps live examples/lights.lps` runs it, but there is
 * nothing to click: the mouse events come from the viewer.
 */

fluents  lamp(_, _).
events   lps_mousedown(_, _, _), lps_mouseup(_, _, _), lps_mousedrag(_, _, _).
actions  toggle(_), warn(_).

initially lamp(1, on), lamp(2, off), lamp(3, on), lamp(4, off).

%  Which lamp is under the pointer. The scene puts lamp N in a 60-wide cell
%  starting at x = (N-1)*70, so this is the inverse of the layout below —
%  ordinary Prolog, evaluated when the event arrives.
lamp_at(X, N) :- N0 is X // 70, N is N0 + 1, N >= 1, N =< 4.

%  Two rules for one click, and the difference between them is the whole point
%  of §8 of the tutorial: the *condition* is the intent, and it can be wrong;
%  the *constraint* below is the guarantee, and it cannot.
if   lps_mousedown(X, _, _) from _ to T1, lamp_at(X, N), not only_one_on(N) at T1
then toggle(N) from T1 to T2.

if   lps_mousedown(X, _, _) from _ to T1, lamp_at(X, N), only_one_on(N) at T1
then warn(that_is_the_last_lamp) from T1 to T2.

%  One causal law, not two. Two — `updates on to off` and `updates off to on`
%  — both fire on the same cycle and toggle the lamp back again, which looks
%  exactly like the click doing nothing.
toggle(N) updates Old to New in lamp(N, Old) if flip(Old, New).

flip(on, off).
flip(off, on).

%  Two intensional fluents: derived whenever they are asked for, never stored.
%  `not` is LPS's negation in a condition — Prolog's `\+` around a timed
%  literal is not the same thing and does not compile.
another_on(N) at T if lamp(M, on) at T, M \= N.
only_one_on(N) at T if lamp(N, on) at T, not another_on(N) at T.

%  The rule that makes this worth clicking on: you cannot turn the last lamp
%  off. Not because the click handler is careful — because the engine refuses
%  the action, and would refuse it however the toggle was asked for.
%  Remove the guard from the rule above and this still holds: the engine will
%  not commit the action, whoever asks and however they ask.
false toggle(N) from T1 to _, only_one_on(N) at T1.

/*  The scene. Lamps in a row, on the same grid `lamp_at/2` inverts — a scene
    and its hit test have to agree, and the only way to be sure is to write
    them next to each other. */
display(lamp(N, on),  [type:raster, icon:bulb, position:[X, 40], scale:0.55]) :- X is (N-1)*70 + 8.
display(lamp(N, off), [type:rectangle, from:[X, 40], to:[X2, 100],
		       fillColor:'#2a2f3a', strokeColor:'#4a5162', label:N]) :-
	X is (N-1)*70 + 8, X2 is X + 52.
display(timeless, [
	[type:rectangle, from:[-6, -6], to:[286, 130], strokeColor:'#2a2f3a'],
	[type:text, point:[8, 112], content:'click a lamp', fontSize:13, fillColor:'#8b94a6']
	]).
